# WHY THE BUILD DESCRIPTION IS NOT IN HERE.
#
# GoogleTest ships a CMakeLists.txt, so vendoring it is a copy and an
# add_subdirectory. libsodium ships autotools and nothing else. Running
# `configure` would be a build step that runs a shell, probes the compiler and
# WRITES INTO THIS TREE -- after which this directory would no longer match the
# hash below, and the check that makes vendoring auditable would be gone.
#
# So the CMake for it lives at third_party/libsodium.cmake, one level up. This
# tree is never written to, including by a build. See that file for what is
# compiled and for why the portable (non-SIMD) paths are the ones selected.

# WHAT IS USED, AND WHAT THE REST IS DOING HERE.
#
# Umbra calls crypto_generichash (BLAKE2b) and sodium_init, and nothing else
# yet. The whole tree is vendored anyway rather than the subset those need,
# because sodium_init initializes several primitives and the "minimal" cut would
# be a hand-maintained list that drifts on every pin bump. More importantly, a
# subset cannot be checked against the hash below, and that check is the point:
# a partial copy has to be audited by reading it, a whole one by comparing one
# number. Later phases need the AEAD and the KDF regardless.

# THE ONE FILE THAT IS NOT UPSTREAM'S
#
# This file, and only this file. To confirm the rest is upstream's, recompute
# the tree hash with this file excluded and compare against `tree:` above:
#
#   git rev-parse HEAD:third_party/libsodium      # with VERSION removed
#
# The equality is the whole check; excluding one hard-coded name costs nothing
# because the equality itself guards the exclusion.

exclude-from-hash: VERSION
